    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We start by setting up an inequality to represent the given condition. Let \\( x \\) be the integer whose square is less than its double. This can be written as:\n\n\\[ x^2 < 2x \\]\n\nNext, we rearrange the inequality to bring all terms to one side:\n\n\\[ x^2 - 2x < 0 \\]\n\nWe can factor out \\( x \\) from the left-hand side:\n\n\\[ x(x - 2) < 0 \\]\n\nTo solve this inequality, we need to determine where the product \\( x(x - 2) \\) is negative. We do this by analyzing the critical points where the expression changes sign. These critical points occur when \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( x = 2 \\). These points divide the number line into three intervals: \\( (-\\infty, 0) \\), \\( (0, 2) \\), and \\( (2, \\infty) \\).\n\nWe test a point in each interval to determine the sign of the expression \\( x(x - 2) \\) in that interval:\n\n1. For \\( x \\in (-\\infty, 0) \\), choose \\( x = -1 \\):\n   \\[ (-1)(-1 - 2) = (-1)(-3) = 3 > 0 \\]\n   So, \\( x(x - 2) \\) is positive in this interval.\n\n2. For \\( x \\in (0, 2) \\), choose \\( x = 1 \\):\n   \\[ (1)(1 - 2) = (1)(-1) = -1 < 0 \\]\n   So, \\( x(x - 2) \\) is negative in this interval.\n\n3. For \\( x \\in (2, \\infty) \\), choose \\( x = 3 \\):\n   \\[ (3)(3 - 2) = (3)(1) = 3 > 0 \\]\n   So, \\( x(x - 2) \\) is positive in this interval.\n\nThus, the expression \\( x(x - 2) \\) is negative in the interval \\( (0, 2) \\). Since we are looking for integer solutions, the only integer in this interval is \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nTherefore, the only integer whose square is less than its double is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
